# 🌸 Linux 7.3 Tulip

**Linux, carefully cultivated.**

Built from upstream Linux. Hardened conservatively. Validated honestly.

Tulip is not a replacement for Linux and does not claim authorship of the Linux
kernel. It is an engineering effort built directly on upstream Linux, using
carefully selected configuration, hardening, trust, memory, testing and release
policies.

## Current status

- Upstream baseline: Linux v7.3
- Baseline commit: c03c1a14507dfaf8b856c2608f9ac216021fc222
- Tulip release tag: v7.3-tulip1
- Release commit: 3a4ae31d5515b378e08a907ee6618eca185ef953
- Current engineering state: M1-M20 PASS VERIFIED
- Project mode: MAINTENANCE
- Linux 7.4 migration: not started

## What is Tulip?

Tulip carefully builds an opinionated, hardened and auditable kernel profile
around upstream Linux. It values upstream compatibility, conservative
engineering, security hardening, memory efficiency, trust and integrity,
recovery, reproducibility, and evidence-driven testing.

## Key features

- Conservative default hardening (TulipGuard)
- Trust and integrity capabilities (TulipTrust)
- MGLRU memory baseline
- Optional LowMem zram capability
- Recovery and fallback design
- QEMU compatibility validation
- Reproducible build documentation

## Tulip Memory

Architecture:

- MGLRU = standard baseline
- zram = optional LowMem capability
- zswap = not forced
- DAMON = research/debug

LowMem policy:

- <=4GB: MGLRU + zram recommended as guidance
- 4-8GB: MGLRU default, zram optional
- >8GB: MGLRU default, zram normally unnecessary

This policy is conservative guidance, not a claim of universal performance
superiority.

## TulipGuard

TulipGuard enables a conservative set of upstream Linux hardening capabilities
intended to raise the difficulty of exploiting certain classes of kernel defects.
It is not a claim of immunity.

## TulipTrust

TulipTrust provides trust capabilities, including module signing, fs-verity and
dm-verity support. CAPABILITY is distinct from mandatory ENFORCEMENT POLICY.

## Compatibility

Tulip uses normal Linux driver, firmware and userspace mechanisms. QEMU testing
has validated selected virtual configurations. Physical hardware requires its
own validation. Tulip does not claim support for every PC or GPU.

